summary: "Plot supplies the camera, a coordinate frame, and settings. Add a mark inside it to turn data into a picture."
---

## Install for your environment

For Svelte 5, install hmplot with npm and import components from the package. In SvelteKit, render the plot in the browser. For Python, install hmplot in the notebook kernel environment; evaluating a root Plot displays its anywidget view. You do not need a Scene for a single plot. Both frontends render using WebGPU, so use a supported browser or notebook webview.

```text
npm install hmplot
```

```text
python -m pip install hmplot
```

## Read the example

The table contains equal-length columns. Each row becomes one point. The strings x and y name columns; size=8 is a constant in screen pixels. Plot infers the positional domains from its data. Svelte uses nested markup; Python uses a with block to attach marks to their parent. Exiting the Python block finishes construction; the final plot expression displays it.

## Try one change

Set size to 14. The points become larger without changing their data coordinates. Add fill="group" to color rows by category. Next, learn which component owns data, scales, and the camera.

```python example
import hmplot as hm

data = {"x":[1,2,3,4,5,6],"y":[2,4,3,6,5,7],"group":["A","A","A","B","B","B"],"score":[10,25,40,60,75,95]}

with hm.Plot(data=data, height=340, axes2d=True) as plot:
    hm.Points(x="x", y="y", size=4)

plot
```

```svelte
<script>
  import { Plot, Points } from "hmplot";
  const data = {"x":[1,2,3,4,5,6],"y":[2,4,3,6,5,7],"group":["A","A","A","B","B","B"],"score":[10,25,40,60,75,95]};
</script>

<Plot {data} height={340} axes2d>
  <Points x="x" y="y" size={4} />
</Plot>
```
